        "worked_solution": "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
